{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZtRx8VkvDnWseAxxAgY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate, and the self-check printed **OK**. On its short smoke fit the model's ELPD-LOO was −974.5, close to the incumbent's −973.3. That comparison isn't reliable yet, for three reasons:\n\n- The smoke fit was only 100 draws on one chain.\n- It flagged PSIS-LOO as unreliable: 5% of trials had high Pareto-k, against a 1% tolerance.\n- It couldn't check convergence, since one chain can't report R-hat.\n\nAdmission's full fit will settle it. The check also doesn't test novelty against the other models.\n\n**Model name:** `lapse_ideal_alternation_streak_penalty`\n\n**What it refines:** I chose `ideal_alternation_streak_penalty`, which has lost to the incumbent by 36.1 ± 12.9 nats (2.8× dse). It keeps its mechanism:\n- each person has their own ideal switching rate and prefers the sequence whose alternation rate is closer to it;\n- everyone shares a penalty on the longest streak of identical flips.\n\n**The one change:** I added the incumbent's decision rule. On some trials a person guesses instead of comparing, and how often varies from person to person. The idea is that the streak and alternation preferences are then estimated from the people who actually use them, not flattened by indifferent ones.\n\n**Why:** It targets the critique's one discrepancy that survived the FDR correction. Among pairs with similar alternation rates, people pick the sequence with the shorter longest run more often than the incumbent predicts: 0.688 observed vs 0.615 predicted, q = 0.016.\n\nThe novelty gate might reject it as too close to a live model. The incumbent's own refinement slots could produce a similar model by adding a streak penalty to it.\n\nFiles are in `experiment1/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_5/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"sto